{
  "name": "standard",
  "description": "Default temperate dressing. GROUND VEGETATION CARRIES THE MAP; trees are accents on top of it. Previously every green thing was a 6-9 m tree over a bare floor, which at the battle camera is a field of lollipops over nothing - and the grass tufts meant to cover that floor were 1.1 m across, did not resolve at that distance, and were dropped rather than fixed. brush_mat replaces them at 3-4 m across, so one instance does the work of a dozen tufts. Layer order is also the order the global ceiling is spent, so the ground layers come FIRST and the trees take what is left.",
  "_conditions": [
    "Every layer's `where` block is a set of ranges. Omit a key, or set it to",
    "null, and that condition does not apply.",
    "  slope           0 = flat, 0.70 = the walkable limit (MAX_WALKABLE_SLOPE)",
    "  height          world units; the map's own vertical range",
    "  aspect          [centre_degrees, tolerance] - 0 N, 90 E, 180 S, 270 W.",
    "                  CIRCULAR, so [0, 60] means 300..60 through north.",
    "                  Flat ground has no aspect and matches no aspect rule.",
    "  water_distance  world units to the nearest water edge; huge when dry",
    "  curvature       -1 hollow, 0 planar, +1 convex ridge or rim",
    "Layer order is draw order and ceiling order; conditions decide placement."
  ],
  "avoid_radius": 16.0,
